{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-148288",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{x}}-1\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}-1.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}-1 \\\\ x+1 & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+1} \\\\ (\\sqrt{y})^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{x+1})}^{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: The domain and range of $f(x)$ are given by\n\n$$\nx>0,\\,f(x)>-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain and range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ are\n\n$$\nx>-1,\\,{f}^{-1}(x)>0.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
